ets price Green's chances near 9 percent, so a Likely R rating with a single-digit Republican margin fits the evidence.", "watch": "Any credible general-election polling showing Green within single digits, or a shift in the national environment toward Democrats, would move this toward Lean R.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Open seat: Neal Dunn (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Neal Dunn's retirement leaves an open Republican-leaning Panhandle seat, with Republican Austin Rogers facing Democrat Amanda Green in November. The district carries an R+8 partisan lean, and the 2026 national environment has favored Republicans, giving Rogers a structural advantage despite the lack of an incumbent on the ballot.\n\nMoney tells a similar story: Rogers has raised over $1 million and benefits from roughly $1.4 million in outside support, while Green has raised about $350,000 with little outside help. Expert raters overwhelmingly place the race in the Solid or Safe Republican column, though a few see it as merely Likely or Lean R. Prediction markets give Green roughly a one-in-ten chance. With no reliable general-election polls available, the forecast rests on fundamentals, ratings, and spending, all of which point to a Republican hold in the mid-single to low-double digits.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0837, "margin": -8.48}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.084, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.1162, "margin": -8.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.1045, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.0789, "margin": -9.0}]}